---
// /coaching - The 1:1 Program (+ $97 intro session entry)
// Copy base: old /premium-coaching page (canon), per one-on-one-page-spec.md rev 2026-07-11.
// Surgical changes only: typo fixes, "slave to nicotine" swap, one Program replaces
// two packages, no strikethrough pricing, no timeline claims.
// DIFFS FOR JON'S REVIEW are marked [DIFF] in comments.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';

// $97 session sells via its own standalone landing page (decided 2026-07-12).
const SESSION_URL = 'https://abq-landing-v2.vercel.app';

// The $1,200 Program checkout (decided 2026-08-18, Jon): a Stripe Payment Link.
// Jon then issues a Cal.com Private Link (6 uses, expires 12 weeks from purchase)
// by hand. Calendly Meeting Packages were evaluated and rejected - Jon stays on
// Cal.com. See PRD "Program checkout - DECIDED" for the full flow.
// Allowed off-domain under PRD site-rule 2 (checkout is the stated exception).
//
// While it's empty both Program CTAs fall back to /contact, so someone ready to
// spend $1,200 reaches Jon instead of hitting a dead link. They previously
// pointed at "#book", which is the id of the final CTA section itself - i.e.
// the button scrolled to its own location and nothing happened.
//
// ✅ LIVE PAYMENT LINK (Jon, 2026-08-25). Created 2026-08-21, verified live:
// $1,200, correct description, promo codes on, no tax row. THIS TAKES REAL
// MONEY - test cards like 4242 4242 4242 4242 are declined here, and any
// checkout completed against it is a real charge with a real Stripe fee.
//
// ⚠️ Open item carried over from SETUP.md: the terms checkbox on THIS link was
// never confirmed, because Stripe Link intercepted the payment panel during
// verification. Check it via "Pay without Link". The community link was fully
// verified; this one was not.
const PROGRAM_URL = 'https://buy.stripe.com/14A6oAf6Rddje5w9mR2oE00';
const PROGRAM_CTA = PROGRAM_URL || '/contact';

// Build-time guard: shipping a test link to production would silently take the
// site's only $1,200 CTA out of service. Astro frontmatter runs at build time,
// so this lands in the build log where it is hard to miss.
if (PROGRAM_URL.includes('/test_')) {
  console.warn(
    '\n⚠️  /coaching is using a STRIPE SANDBOX link (test_...). ' +
      'It cannot accept real payments. Replace PROGRAM_URL before launch.\n'
  );
}
---

<Base
  title="1:1 Coaching: Quit for the Last Time | Always Be Quitting"
  description="Private 1:1 coaching with Jon Fritsch, CTTS & NCNTT certified tobacco treatment specialist. Start with a $97 intro session."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  {/* hero: headline + video slot + trust line. [DIFF] H1 uses program name instead of "That Actually Works" */}
  <header class="band band--paper hero" style="padding-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem)">
    <div class="wrap">
      {/* H1 revised 2026-08-23 for search. Was "Quit for the Last Time: 1:1
          coaching with Jon" - carried "quit" but not "smoking or vaping", so it
          missed the terms people actually search ("quit smoking coaching").
          Jon's program name is preserved intact. */}
      <h1 class="display-xl hl">
        Quit Smoking or Vaping for the Last Time: <em>1:1 coaching with Jon</em>
      </h1>
      {/* Self-hosted, click-to-load. Encoded from the 515MB / 15.2 Mbps master
           down to 27MB / 805 kbps. TODO (PRD #15): move files to R2. */}
      <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: var(--gap-md); max-width:720px; margin-inline:auto">
        <VideoEmbed
          src720="/video/coaching-720p.mp4"
          src480="/video/coaching-480p.mp4"
          poster="/video/coaching-poster.webp"
          posterMobile="/video/coaching-poster-480.webp"
          posterFallback="/video/coaching-poster.jpg"
          title="Why 1:1 coaching is right for you"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* compressed pitch: the killer lines, excavated from the four paragraphs [DIFF: compression only, no new words except connectives] */}
  <section class="band band--white" style="padding-block: var(--section)">
    <div class="wrap wrap--read">
      <h2 class="display-lg hl">This is not just <em>another quit attempt.</em></h2>
      <div class="pitch reveal">
        <p>
          I transform people into non-smokers and non-vapers using proven coaching techniques I've developed over
          25 years. The "magic formula" isn't a formula - it's expert guidance, accountability, and flexibility
          from someone who knows withdrawal symptom management and impactful behavior change techniques.
        </p>
        <p>
          You don't need to have it all figured out or be "ready enough" - that's what I'm here for.
        </p>
        <p>
          Quitting nicotine is one of the hardest things you will ever do. I've never seen a perfect quitting
          attempt. Coaching makes it easier, with less struggling and more success.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>I'm all-in with my clients. If you're ready to commit to changing your life, let's work together.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* TITLES REWRITTEN 2026-08-25 (Jon). BODIES ARE STILL THE VERBATIM LIVE-SITE
       BULLETS - only the eight h3s changed, plus one word in the scheduling card.

       WHY. Jon: the section "is just boring to look at ... supposed to be
       scannable and it's just not really even worth looking at." Scanning means
       reading the titles, and the old ones were a taxonomy: "Fluid quit plan",
       "Advanced mindset focus", "Whole-life approach". Every actual reason to
       buy sat in the body, so a scanner got the filing system and missed the
       goods. Design can enlarge these and separate them from the body and it
       still would not be worth stopping on - a labelling problem, not a
       styling one, which is why it was fixed in the copy phase.

       NOTHING WAS CUT OR MERGED. I proposed dropping the scheduling card as
       logistics and merging 7 into 8 as duplicative. Jon rejected both, and was
       right on both counts:
         - Scheduling stays: flexibility signals a live human service rather
           than, in his words, "a paint by numbers course". At $1,200 against a
           $30 app that IS the differentiator, not friction-removal.
         - 7 and 8 stay separate: withdrawal symptoms connect to both, and they
           cover different domains. 7 is the STRUCTURE of your life - work,
           relationships, daily routines, goals. 8 is HOW YOU FEEL day to day -
           sleep, stress, weight, emotions. Relationships legitimately appear
           in both. CORRECTION 2026-08-25: an earlier version of this note
           claimed the two run in opposite directions (life -> quit, then quit
           -> life). That was wrong - read the bodies, both run life -> quit
           ("life areas that affect your success"). The conclusion to keep them
           separate stands; the reason given for it did not.

       THREE TITLES WERE CORRECTED BY JON 2026-08-25 FOR OVERCLAIMING OR FOR
       SAYING SOMETHING LITERALLY UNTRUE. The pattern is worth learning from:
       claim-carrying titles drift into asserting more than the body supports.
         - "A plan that changes when you do" -> "A plan built around your life".
           Jon: "the person does not change." The plan adapts; the client is
           not the thing being reshaped.
         - "Sessions when you can actually take them" -> "Sessions that fit your
           schedule". Jon: "they are not pills. you don't actively take
           sessions." NOTE the noun: SESSIONS, not meetings. The page reserves
           "Meeting" for the $97 offer and "session" for Program units, so
           swapping the noun here would blur a deliberate distinction.
         - "Your whole life connects to your quit" -> "Work, relationships, and
           routines affect your quit". Jon: "this is a massive overstatement."
           The body only ever claims four domains; the title claimed all of
           life. It now names what the body names, and pairs with card 8 so the
           7/8 split reads at a glance - structure of your life, then how you
           feel day to day.

       ONE WORD TO WATCH: "fit" now appears in card 6's title and body. Card 2
       deliberately says "built around" rather than "fits with" to stop three
       fits landing in one grid.

       TWO TITLES CARRY SPECIFIC REASONING - do not "simplify" either:
         - "You get me, not a program" was nearly changed to "A real person,
           not an app". Jon: that "might be UNDERSTATING the importance of my
           training and ability". Any warm body is a real person; "me" means a
           CTTS/NCNTT specialist with 25 years. Keep the possessive framing.
         - "Everyone deserves to quit, whatever your life looks like" replaced a
           draft reading "Nothing you say is going to shock me". Jon: that
           framing belongs to contraception or STD counselling, where the
           barrier is not being able to say a thing out loud. Quitting is not
           that, and no disclosure would shock him. The real barrier is the
           belief that help is for people who have their act together. His
           words: "I am welcoming and inclusive, and I understand that every
           person deserves to quit no matter what they do with their life."

       MINDSET CARD: FEATURE MINDSET, DO NOT MENTION WILLPOWER, DO NOT
       OVERCLAIM (Jon, 2026-08-25). Three drafts were rejected getting here:
         - "Mindset work, not willpower" - discounted willpower.
         - "Willpower is only part of it" - the overcorrection; made the card
           about willpower instead of mindset. Jon: "I WANT MINDSET TO BE
           FEATURED. DON'T EVEN TALK ABOUT WILLPOWER." The word does not belong
           on this card in any framing, positive or negative.
         - "Deep mindset work, every session" - Jon: "i don't want to claim
           deep mindset work, because that is wrong."
       The shipped line is Jon's own sentence: "Nobody quits without changing
       their mindset. I focus heavily on mindset." It works because it is a
       claim about how QUITTING works, not a boast about how Jon works - which
       is exactly why it clears the no-hype bar that "deep mindset work" fails.
       Keep any replacement on that side of the line. */}
  <section class="band band--paper" style="padding-block: var(--section)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="display-lg">What to Expect From 1:1 Coaching</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top: var(--gap-md)">
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>You get me, not a program</h3><p>Leverage my 25 years of specialized experience in nicotine addiction</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>A plan built around your life</h3><p>Custom strategies that address your specific triggers and patterns</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>You deserve to quit, whatever your life looks like</h3><p>Friendly and non-judgmental with the perfect mix of compassion AND the ability to move you forward</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>You won't be doing this alone</h3><p>Regular check-ins that keep you progressing when motivation wavers</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>Nobody quits without changing their mindset</h3><p>Develop the mental framework that makes quitting permanent</p></div>
        {/* "scheduling" added - without it the line has no subject once the
            title stops saying it. The only body-copy change in the section. */}
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>Sessions that fit your schedule</h3><p>Easy software-based scheduling, with the flexibility to fit your life</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>Work, relationships, and routines affect your quit</h3><p>We address how quitting integrates with your entire lifestyle - how work, relationships, daily routines, and personal goals all connect to quitting</p></div>
        <div class="card reveal"><h3>Help with sleep, stress, and weight, too</h3><p>Sleep improvement, stress management, weight control, emotional challenges, relationship dynamics, and other life areas that affect your success (and generally make you happier)</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* "How it works" mechanics grid REMOVED 2026-08-25 (Jon).

       Six cards on scheduling, video links and file storage. Three of them (1:1
       Sessions, Email Support, Progress Tracking) were already in the offer
       card's prose; two (Easy Scheduling, Simple Video Sessions) were pure
       friction-removal, which nobody weighs at $1,200. It also sat directly
       after the eight "What to Expect" cards, so the reader met fourteen
       near-identical tiles in a row and the page went flat there.

       The Quit Date card was the only one carrying anything unique. BOTH of its
       sentences were moved into FAQ 2 ("I'm not ready to quit yet"), which is
       where a reader with quit-date anxiety actually goes. Nothing was dropped.

       Band sequence was checked before cutting: mist white paper WHITE teal
       paper white mist white teal becomes mist white paper teal paper white
       mist white teal - no two adjacent bands match, so the cut creates no new
       seam problem.

       STILL OPEN, exposed by this cut: nothing on the page says what happens
       after purchase. The real flow is Stripe takes payment, then Jon sends a
       Cal.com private link by hand. The deleted "Easy Scheduling" card implied
       instant self-serve booking. Wants one FAQ item, not six cards - raised
       with Jon 2026-08-25, not yet decided. */}

  {/* pays for itself - verbatim, with the approved swap [DIFF: "slave to nicotine" -> "controlled by nicotine"; duplicate headline bug fixed] */}
  <section class="band band--teal transform" style="text-align:left">
    <div class="wrap wrap--read">
      <h2 class="display-xl hl" style="text-align:center">Premium 1:1 Coaching <em>Pays for Itself</em></h2>
      <div class="pitch reveal" style="margin-top: var(--gap-md)">
        <p>
          The average smoker and vaper spends thousands of dollars every year on cigarettes and vapes, and rarely
          gives it much thought. That steady, outgoing cash flow continues year-after-year with nothing positive
          to show for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          How much did you spend on cigarettes or vapes last year? And the year before? And the year before that?
        </p>
        <p><strong>When you are successful at quitting, you will:</strong></p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>SAVE tens of thousands, if not hundreds of thousands, of dollars in the future.</li>
          <li>Improve your physical health dramatically.</li>
          <li>Stop feeling controlled by nicotine.</li>
          <li>Increase productivity and energy in your professional and personal life.</li>
          <li>Increase your quality of life.</li>
          <li>and dozens of others.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* THE OFFER: Program (gold standard) leads; $97 meeting = the no-commitment option

       "Quit for the Last Time" REMOVED FROM THE OFFER 2026-08-23 (Jon). His
       distinction: as an imperative it is fine - it tells the reader to go and
       do it, and it stays in the H1, the page title and the closing tagline.
       As a PRODUCT NAME sitting beside $1,200 it reads as what the money buys,
       i.e. an outcome promise, which PRD rule 3 forbids.

       The heading now claims the METHOD instead of the result: one-to-one
       coaching is the most intensive form of help available, which is
       defensible and also explains why it costs $1,200. The old heading
       asserted a result without justifying the price.

       Programme is simply "The 1:1 Program" here - already the approved short
       form in offer-architecture.md. Do not reattach the tagline to the price. */}
  <section class="band band--white doors">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="display-lg hl" style="text-align:center">The gold standard in quitting is <em>private, one-to-one, personalized coaching.</em></h2>
      <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top: var(--gap-lg)">
        <div class="door door--community reveal">
          <h3>The 1:1 Program</h3>
          <p>Six private, premium 1:1 coaching sessions with Jon. The most complete, most effective way to quit.</p>
          {/* Qualifier after the figure, matching the $97 card (Jon,
              2026-08-23). Set in a span at 0.42em rather than at full price
              size - "$1,200 6 Session Package" all at 3rem would run to two
              lines. The $97 card gets the same treatment so they stay a pair. */}
          <div class="price">$1,200<span>6 Session Package</span></div>
          <p>Six 50-minute sessions with Jon, one-on-one - a roomy window over 12 weeks, because life happens. Direct email access to Jon between every session. A quit strategy built around your life, expert withdrawal and behavior-change coaching, and progress tracking the whole way.</p>
          <p>Want more time when you're done? Additional sessions are available to purchase whenever more support helps.</p>
          <a class="btn btn--cta" href={PROGRAM_CTA}>GET STARTED NOW!</a>
        </div>
        <div class="door door--session reveal">
          {/* Rewritten 2026-08-14 from the $97 landing page copy. The old version
               sold a DECISION ("the no-risk way to meet Jon and decide for
               yourself"), which framed the session as a consolation prize for the
               undecided. This sells the OUTCOME: you leave with a plan. */}
          <h3>The $97 Special 1:1 Meeting</h3>
          <p>A private 50-minute conversation with Jon, and you can ask me anything. What's kept you stuck. What to try next. Why last time didn't hold. Whether you're ready or nowhere near it.</p>
          <div class="price">$97<span>one-time</span></div>
          {/* "written plan" removed 2026-08-23 (Jon), matching the homepage card:
              it implies a document and a complete quit strategy out of 50
              minutes, which the session cannot reliably deliver. Replaced with
              advice-tailored-to-you, the thing an app or a pamphlet structurally
              cannot offer. Do NOT reintroduce "plan" here. */}
          <p>You'll leave with advice tailored to you - not a one-size-fits-all program - and a clearer read on what's keeping you stuck, what to do next, and how to stop repeating the same quit cycle. No upsells, no subscriptions, and heavily discounted from my normal hourly rate.</p>
          {/* Trimmed 2026-08-23 (Jon). Dropped "so it's worth using well" and
              "Intake form and confirmation sent right away" - the second is
              table stakes, not a benefit worth card space.

              "One per person" STAYS. It is a real constraint the buyer needs
              before booking, not after (offer-architecture rule 6: one $97
              session per person, ever), and the page carries a whole FAQ
              explaining why. Someone who books expecting to re-book later would
              be badly surprised. The homepage card says the same. */}
          <p><strong>One per person.</strong></p>
          <a class="btn btn--cta" href={SESSION_URL}>See what's included</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="pay-for-itself statement reveal">
        Most people spend <strong>$2k, $3k, $4k</strong> on smoking and vaping <em>EVERY YEAR.</em>
        Spend that money on quitting instead. Every ABQ program pays for itself in money saved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Katherine long-form. Heading is LIVE SITE COPY, restored 2026-08-14 after
       being wrongly deleted as "invented filler" - it is Jon's, verbatim.

       ONE TESTIMONIAL, AT FULL LENGTH, AND IT IS HERS - DELIBERATE (Jon,
       2026-08-25), in response to my suggestion that a $1,200 page carrying
       less proof than the homepage was a weakness. It isn't. Jon: "I actually
       pasted Katherine's entire testimonial, and only hers because she directly
       speaks to my ability, our coaching relationship, and says if you want to
       hire a coach hire this guy."

       That is the exact case this page has to make, and she makes it in the
       buyer's own words - see her closing lines: "If you have reservations
       about investing in a coach, I cannot recommend Jon enough." A wall of
       short quotes would not do that job, and adding more would dilute the one
       that does. Do NOT propose adding testimonials here, trimming this one, or
       matching the homepage's three-up treatment. */}
  <section class="band band--paper proof">
    <div class="wrap wrap--read">
      <h2 class="display-lg">How Coaching Made the Difference: Katherine's Experience</h2>
      <figure class="card quote reveal" style="margin-top: var(--gap-md)">
        <blockquote>
          The most difficult aspect of quitting that Jon helped me overcome was personal accountability. As many
          people who vape can relate to, I wanted to quit for years and tried countless times to no avail. I felt
          like I was constantly at war with myself, stuck weighing the pros and cons. On one side, I could ease
          the withdrawal symptoms for a moment by vaping - but at the cost of facing my own disappointment. On
          the other, I was staring down a painful, uncertain journey, unsure if I even had what it took to succeed.
          <br /><br />
          Jon was my outlet, a safe person who let me talk through this internal battle, who shared the weight,
          pressure, and frightening solitude I originally felt in my quit journey. There were many moments
          throughout our work together that I didn't believe I could do it. I was determined to quit vaping but
          terrified of the withdrawal process. Jon was very receptive to these worries; he provided reassuring
          research, perspectives, and options I could take.
          <br /><br />
          What surprised me most about working with Jon was his approach to quitting. I intensified my own worries
          by looking at the whole journey (the rest of my life) at once. Jon not only broke down the process but
          acted as my biggest supporter, always making himself a readily available outlet. I walked away from my
          sessions with confidence, understanding, and ways to move forward.
          <br /><br />
          I believe I was successful this time because of Jon. It took me nearly six years to build the courage to
          find a specialist and I am unbelievably grateful it was Jon. If you have reservations about investing in
          a coach, I cannot recommend Jon enough. The impact he has had on my life is transformative - I have no
          doubt that he can do the same for you.
        </blockquote>
        {/* Photo added 2026-08-23 (Jon). Same asset and treatment as the
            homepage and /community proof sections - .quote__photo comes from
            home.css, and .quote's column-reverse puts it above the quote. */}
        <figcaption>
          <picture>
            <source type="image/webp" srcset="/img/katherine-headshot.webp" />
            <img class="quote__photo" src="/img/katherine-headshot.jpg" width="300" height="300" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Katherine R." />
          </picture>
          <span><b>Katherine R.</b> · One Year Vape Free</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* failure stakes close - verbatim, formatting fixed, offer name updated

       FULL WIDTH, not wrap--read (Jon, 2026-08-23): "when we have one large
       line in a section by itself, it looks better to be in a wider column
       container." Measure is about characters per line, not pixels - at
       display-lg, 800px yields ~25-30 characters and chops the sentence into
       fragments, where the same 800px at body size gives a comfortable 70.

       The identical copy already runs at the default wrap on the homepage; this
       section had an inline 820px that got mapped to the reading width in the
       2026-08-23 collapse. Same words, two measures. */}
  <section class="band band--white" style="padding-block: var(--section); text-align:center">
    <div class="wrap">
      {/* Emphasis MARKUP only - words are verbatim and carry the voice.md
          exception for "physically and mentally sick". Matches the homepage
          version of this same statement, which was styled and this was not. */}
      <h2 class="display-lg statement reveal">
        You can continue to spend <strong>$1k, $2k, $3k, $4k</strong> every year on cigarettes and vapes. You can continue to make
        yourself physically and mentally sick. And next year at this time, <em>you'll wish you had started today.</em>
      </h2>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* FAQ - drafted from Jon's language. APPROVED by Jon 2026-08-14 ("they are
       approved"); the pending-approval marker that sat here was stale. */}
  <section class="band band--paper">
    <div class="wrap faq">
      <h2 class="display-lg">A few things people usually ask.</h2>

      {/* Objection-first ordering: doubt before logistics. At $1,200 people
           hesitate over whether it will work for THEM, not over scheduling.
           Assembled from Jon's own language (live coaching page, community FAQ,
           homepage FAQ, offer-architecture). NO EM DASHES.
           APPROVED BY JON 2026-08-14 - this FAQ is canon copy now. */}

      <details class="reveal">
        <summary>I've tried to quit before and it didn't stick. Why would this be different?</summary>
        <p>
          You weren't missing willpower. You were missing the right support. I've never seen a perfect quitting
          attempt, and most people who quit for good relapsed several times first. That isn't failure, it's the
          path. What changes here is that you stop doing it alone, and you work with someone who has spent 25 years
          learning the many ways people get derailed and the ways through them.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="reveal">
        {/* Last two sentences moved here verbatim from the deleted "Quit Date"
            mechanics card (Jon, 2026-08-25). They answer the question this FAQ
            item raises - if I'm not ready, when do I actually have to stop? -
            and the final clause is the only welcome on the site to someone who
            has already started quitting before buying. Not a timeline promise:
            it describes when a date is SET, hedged with "generally", never when
            someone succeeds. Approved as mechanics in the page spec. */}
        <summary>I'm not ready to quit yet. Should I still do this?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Not being ready is a great reason to start, not a reason to wait. You don't need to have it all
          figured out or be "ready enough", that's what I'm here for. We can spend the early sessions building your
          readiness instead of forcing a quit date you don't believe in. You generally set a quit date in the first
          1–3 weeks. You can also begin having already started quitting.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="reveal">
        <summary>What if I don't quit?</summary>
        <p>
          I'll be straight with you. <strong>What I guarantee</strong> is the delivery: six private sessions, a
          quit strategy built around your life, and direct email access to me between sessions. You also get
          25 years of specialized experience in nicotine addiction working on your quit, and my word that <strong>I'm all-in with my clients.</strong>
          <strong>What I can't guarantee</strong> is that you'll quit. Your success depends on factors under your
          control: your mindset, your decisions, your commitment, and how you navigate what comes up. Like any
          coach, I provide guidance, accountability and support, but I can't execute the process for you. I think
          you can do this.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="reveal">
        <summary>Is it worth $1,200?</summary>
        <p>
          Most people spend $2k, $3k, $4k every year on cigarettes and vapes and rarely give it much thought. That
          money goes out year after year with nothing positive to show for it. Quitting pays for itself, and then
          it keeps paying: tens of thousands, possibly hundreds of thousands, over the rest of your life. You're
          already spending the money. You're just giving it to the tobacco companies, with no end in sight.
        </p>
      </details>

      {/* Added 2026-08-25 (Jon): "it wouldn't hurt to make a FAQ out of what
           would happen. Besides that I don't think it needs to be featured
           anywhere. Most people know when they pay for a service, the service
           will tell them how to then access the service." So it lives here and
           nowhere else - it replaces nothing and is not promoted on the page.

           Placed directly before "after the six sessions" so the two run in
           chronological order: purchase, then sessions, then what follows.

           🚨 EVERY FACT HERE MIRRORS WHAT STRIPE ALREADY TELLS THE BUYER. Do not
           reword one without the others. Sources in SETUP.md:
             - confirmation message: "I'll email your personal booking link
